WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:06.375
&gt;&gt; All right. Thanks
everybody for

00:00:06.376 --> 00:00:08.590
coming back to our second panel,

00:00:08.591 --> 00:00:10.100
it was so much fun
in the first panel,

00:00:10.101 --> 00:00:13.020
I can't wait to see what
happens for the second one.

00:00:13.021 --> 00:00:15.269
We're going to be hearing about

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:17.130
the frontiers of
machine learning,

00:00:17.131 --> 00:00:19.080
which is a great segue
I think from

00:00:19.081 --> 00:00:21.810
the topics that were
discussed in the last panel.

00:00:21.811 --> 00:00:25.735
The moderator of this panel
is Kevin Leyton-Brown.

00:00:25.736 --> 00:00:27.600
Kevin is a professor at

00:00:27.601 --> 00:00:29.790
the University of
British Columbia and has spent

00:00:29.791 --> 00:00:32.070
some time in the lab
on sabbatical,

00:00:32.071 --> 00:00:35.920
so we all really
appreciate his expertise.

00:00:35.921 --> 00:00:37.860
He has a lot of work at

00:00:37.861 --> 00:00:41.565
the intersection of machine
learning and economics.

00:00:41.566 --> 00:00:45.275
He has won the Edelman prize

00:00:45.276 --> 00:00:48.885
for his work in
the FCC Spectrum options,

00:00:48.886 --> 00:00:51.140
which he designed along with

00:00:51.141 --> 00:00:53.630
his students and
Paul Milgrom to sell

00:00:53.631 --> 00:00:58.504
spectra for the auctions,

00:00:58.505 --> 00:01:00.375
the recent spectrum auctions.

00:01:00.376 --> 00:01:04.305
So, with that, I'd like to
introduce Kevin Leyton-Brown.

00:01:04.306 --> 00:01:12.110
&gt;&gt; Thanks very much. What's
really a joy to be here at

00:01:12.111 --> 00:01:15.320
one of the great
meeting places in

00:01:15.321 --> 00:01:19.625
our field for a really
interdisciplinary top-notch work.

00:01:19.626 --> 00:01:22.370
Thank you so much for
having me and it's

00:01:22.371 --> 00:01:25.250
also a joy to be here with
this distinguished panel.

00:01:25.251 --> 00:01:27.380
So, I'd like to begin by asking

00:01:27.381 --> 00:01:30.675
each member of the panel to
briefly introduce themselves,

00:01:30.676 --> 00:01:34.850
briefly, and say something

00:01:34.851 --> 00:01:36.410
about their affiliation and

00:01:36.411 --> 00:01:37.610
their connection to
machine learning,

00:01:37.611 --> 00:01:39.025
and then we'll dive in.

00:01:39.026 --> 00:01:41.450
&gt;&gt; Hi. I'm Animoto Kumar.

00:01:41.451 --> 00:01:43.260
I'm a professor at Caltech as

00:01:43.261 --> 00:01:45.595
well as director of
machine learning.

00:01:45.596 --> 00:01:47.925
Sorry, can you hear me now?

00:01:47.926 --> 00:01:49.770
Hi. I'm Anima Anandkumar.

00:01:49.771 --> 00:01:52.550
I'm a professor at Caltech
as well as the director of

00:01:52.551 --> 00:01:55.850
Machine Learning
Research at Enridia.

00:01:55.851 --> 00:01:58.220
I work at the intersections
of theory and

00:01:58.221 --> 00:02:00.610
practice of machine learning.

00:02:00.611 --> 00:02:03.830
I have a lot of
connections to this lab,

00:02:03.831 --> 00:02:05.600
but this has been

00:02:05.601 --> 00:02:11.150
such a nurturing atmosphere
in prime stages of my career.

00:02:11.151 --> 00:02:14.825
Jennifer and Christian
have been amazing mentors.

00:02:14.826 --> 00:02:17.000
Some of the early work,

00:02:17.001 --> 00:02:18.560
the first work I did on

00:02:18.561 --> 00:02:22.435
my core area of research
on tensors started here,

00:02:22.436 --> 00:02:24.540
right in this building.

00:02:24.541 --> 00:02:28.030
So, a lot of great
memories of this place

00:02:28.031 --> 00:02:29.810
and it's amazing that it's

00:02:29.811 --> 00:02:33.500
been 10 years. Congratulations.

00:02:33.501 --> 00:02:35.980
&gt;&gt; So, I'm Victor.

00:02:35.981 --> 00:02:38.775
&gt;&gt; We can't hear you
too. The mic is not on.

00:02:38.776 --> 00:02:40.730
&gt;&gt; We're going to see whether
the learning panelists

00:02:40.731 --> 00:02:43.040
can learn to turn their mics on.

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:46.010
We've had two training
examples now.

00:02:46.011 --> 00:02:48.260
&gt;&gt; Is it on? Can you hear me?

00:02:48.261 --> 00:02:49.835
&gt;&gt; No.

00:02:49.836 --> 00:02:51.050
&gt;&gt; Is it on?

00:02:51.051 --> 00:02:51.920
&gt;&gt; It's on.

00:02:51.921 --> 00:02:53.620
&gt;&gt; Okay. While we
sort out that mic,

00:02:53.621 --> 00:02:54.730
let's keep moving down

00:02:54.731 --> 00:02:56.050
the line and we'll
come back to you.

00:02:56.051 --> 00:02:56.845
&gt;&gt; Okay, yes.

00:02:56.846 --> 00:02:58.120
&gt;&gt; All right, hello.

00:02:58.121 --> 00:03:02.870
&gt;&gt; You bring it closer,
can you try that?

00:03:02.871 --> 00:03:05.760
Now, I think it's on because
I hear it making noise.

00:03:05.761 --> 00:03:08.805
I think we'll bring it closer.

00:03:08.806 --> 00:03:10.350
&gt;&gt; Maybe I have to shout.

00:03:10.351 --> 00:03:11.070
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:03:11.071 --> 00:03:12.840
&gt;&gt; I think the mics
are quite directional,

00:03:12.841 --> 00:03:14.010
so if you have it
off to the side,

00:03:14.011 --> 00:03:14.920
you're going to
have to be careful

00:03:14.921 --> 00:03:16.990
not to look the other way.

00:03:17.990 --> 00:03:20.090
It's a big group,
I know people in

00:03:20.091 --> 00:03:21.970
the back really appreciate
the mics working.

00:03:21.971 --> 00:03:29.700
Maybe you just hold it.

00:03:29.701 --> 00:03:30.780
&gt;&gt; Yes, okay, yes.

00:03:30.781 --> 00:03:31.290
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:03:31.291 --> 00:03:31.830
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:03:31.831 --> 00:03:35.580
&gt;&gt; So, I'm [inaudible].

00:03:35.581 --> 00:03:41.430
My main job is I work on,

00:03:41.431 --> 00:03:43.780
I come from the MIT
economics department.

00:03:43.781 --> 00:03:44.980
I also represent here

00:03:44.981 --> 00:03:47.540
the Center for statistics
and data science,

00:03:47.541 --> 00:03:51.080
which was created
several years ago.

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:56.670
I have been involved in
creation of various programs,

00:03:56.671 --> 00:03:59.320
education programs,
and statistics.

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:01.620
My field is econometrics,

00:04:01.621 --> 00:04:03.370
which are defined
as the intersection

00:04:03.371 --> 00:04:05.385
of economics with
machine learning.

00:04:05.386 --> 00:04:10.005
The field exists like it's
a 100 years old or so.

00:04:10.006 --> 00:04:13.020
At the beginning,

00:04:13.021 --> 00:04:16.590
the roots of the field

00:04:16.591 --> 00:04:19.950
were always involved with
causal inference. The early.

00:04:19.951 --> 00:04:25.440
&gt;&gt; I think your mic
is not working.

00:04:25.441 --> 00:04:26.985
&gt;&gt; Let him try.

00:04:26.986 --> 00:04:28.690
&gt;&gt; Yes?

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:30.360
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:04:30.361 --> 00:04:33.390
&gt;&gt; So, the first incantations

00:04:33.391 --> 00:04:35.730
started to work on inference,

00:04:35.731 --> 00:04:37.980
focus on problems, it was

00:04:37.981 --> 00:04:39.710
in the context of
supply and demand,

00:04:39.711 --> 00:04:42.300
and the field is defined by this.

00:04:42.301 --> 00:04:45.000
To this day, this is one
of the most interesting,

00:04:45.001 --> 00:04:49.600
exciting topics and areas
in machine learning.

00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:53.565
I'll do a bit of
work in that area,

00:04:53.566 --> 00:04:57.805
so some of the work of

00:04:57.806 --> 00:05:01.060
the most exciting work for
my personal work I did

00:05:01.061 --> 00:05:02.750
actually while visiting here

00:05:02.751 --> 00:05:04.835
at Microsoft a
couple of years ago.

00:05:04.836 --> 00:05:06.110
Some of this work with device

00:05:06.111 --> 00:05:07.340
machine learning for
causal inference,

00:05:07.341 --> 00:05:09.410
I did actually here in the labs,

00:05:09.411 --> 00:05:12.350
I really appreciate it.

00:05:12.351 --> 00:05:13.760
&gt;&gt; Hi, everyone.
I'm Lester Mackey.

00:05:13.761 --> 00:05:16.250
I'm one of the newest
researchers here at

00:05:16.251 --> 00:05:19.185
Microsoft Research,
New England. Hello.

00:05:19.186 --> 00:05:20.415
&gt;&gt; They're switching you on.

00:05:20.416 --> 00:05:21.910
&gt;&gt; Wow.

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:24.615
&gt;&gt; Can you hear me now?

00:05:24.616 --> 00:05:26.220
&gt;&gt; Yes, that's.

00:05:26.221 --> 00:05:29.370
&gt;&gt; Good. I'm Lester Mackey.

00:05:29.371 --> 00:05:30.810
I'm one of the newest researchers

00:05:30.811 --> 00:05:32.220
here at Microsoft
Research, New England.

00:05:32.221 --> 00:05:33.675
I joined two years ago.

00:05:33.676 --> 00:05:35.390
Before that, I was
a professor at Stanford

00:05:35.391 --> 00:05:37.185
in statistics and
computer science.

00:05:37.186 --> 00:05:38.865
My background's machine learning.

00:05:38.866 --> 00:05:41.860
I work on methodology,
theory, modeling.

00:05:41.861 --> 00:05:43.290
A lot of the problems I work on

00:05:43.291 --> 00:05:45.245
these days stem from health care,

00:05:45.246 --> 00:05:47.835
some weather and
climate forecasting,

00:05:47.836 --> 00:05:49.345
high-energy physics,

00:05:49.346 --> 00:05:53.395
the social good more broadly.
So, I'm glad to be here.

00:05:53.396 --> 00:05:58.020
&gt;&gt; All right. Does my microphone
work out of the box?

00:05:58.021 --> 00:06:01.250
They seem to be sabotaging
everybody else.

00:06:01.251 --> 00:06:02.570
I'm trying to move away from

00:06:02.571 --> 00:06:04.505
Lester because I think
his microphone is picking me up.

00:06:04.506 --> 00:06:06.710
&gt;&gt; Sorry about that.

00:06:06.711 --> 00:06:08.270
So, I'm Nicco Afuzi.
I'm a researcher

00:06:08.271 --> 00:06:09.900
here in New England.

00:06:09.901 --> 00:06:11.860
I joined this lab
now three years ago,

00:06:11.861 --> 00:06:14.670
which seems longer.

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:19.570
As we were saying
last night at dinner,

00:06:19.571 --> 00:06:21.435
time moves fast
when you have fun.

00:06:21.436 --> 00:06:22.750
I've been having
fun, but still it

00:06:22.751 --> 00:06:24.400
feels like more than three years.

00:06:24.401 --> 00:06:25.540
&gt;&gt; You've done a lot.

00:06:25.541 --> 00:06:27.924
&gt;&gt; Done some.

00:06:27.925 --> 00:06:31.420
I'm a machine learning
researcher by training.

00:06:31.421 --> 00:06:33.700
I got very interested in
computational biology,

00:06:33.701 --> 00:06:36.089
so I worked a lot on
statistical genetics,

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:38.830
genome-wide association
studies methodology.

00:06:38.831 --> 00:06:40.600
Then, I move to
functional genomics

00:06:40.601 --> 00:06:42.160
with crisper gene editing.

00:06:42.161 --> 00:06:44.620
Then, I went back to

00:06:44.621 --> 00:06:45.970
machine learning working

00:06:45.971 --> 00:06:48.070
on high-dimensional
busy optimizations,

00:06:48.071 --> 00:06:50.740
which as you know under

00:06:50.741 --> 00:06:52.060
the name has been rocked under

00:06:52.061 --> 00:06:54.595
name of automated
machine learning now.

00:06:54.596 --> 00:06:57.580
I think that the New
England lab is one of

00:06:57.581 --> 00:07:00.200
the few places in the world
where one can move

00:07:00.201 --> 00:07:02.720
from statistical genetics
to then doing some

00:07:02.721 --> 00:07:03.830
crisper gene editing using

00:07:03.831 --> 00:07:05.780
machine learning to
improve that obviously,

00:07:05.781 --> 00:07:07.580
and then moving back
to machine learning

00:07:07.581 --> 00:07:10.770
without serious
career consequences.

00:07:10.771 --> 00:07:13.750
In fact, like moving
from field to field is,

00:07:13.751 --> 00:07:15.185
I feel like it's encouraged.

00:07:15.186 --> 00:07:18.850
So, yes, t's great to be here.

00:07:18.851 --> 00:07:20.700
&gt;&gt; All right. Thanks everybody.

00:07:20.701 --> 00:07:22.070
So, as you guys can see,

00:07:22.071 --> 00:07:23.690
we're already modelling to

00:07:23.691 --> 00:07:25.130
you the way we
intend to continue,

00:07:25.131 --> 00:07:27.580
which is to be very informal.

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:31.830
Hopefully, maybe with
working microphones from now on.

00:07:31.831 --> 00:07:33.860
So, we're going to
encourage the panelists

00:07:33.861 --> 00:07:35.890
to make brief statements,

00:07:35.891 --> 00:07:38.640
jump in as they see
fit on each question

00:07:38.641 --> 00:07:39.690
to speak more than once to

00:07:39.691 --> 00:07:41.605
each question if they so choose.

00:07:41.606 --> 00:07:43.650
So, don't think that people are

00:07:43.651 --> 00:07:45.730
being unruly and bold
when they do this,

00:07:45.731 --> 00:07:47.865
this is the way we've
asked them to do it.

00:07:47.866 --> 00:07:49.990
So, we're meant to be talking

00:07:49.991 --> 00:07:51.985
about the frontiers
of machine learning.

00:07:51.986 --> 00:07:54.660
It's no surprise that to any

00:07:54.661 --> 00:07:57.185
of you here that machine
learning is having a moment

00:07:57.186 --> 00:07:59.515
that computer science is
suddenly finding that

00:07:59.516 --> 00:08:02.310
machine learning is an animating
principle of the decade,

00:08:02.311 --> 00:08:05.990
and that so much is changing
about the way we work with

00:08:05.991 --> 00:08:07.950
data and the way
systems are able to be

00:08:07.951 --> 00:08:10.210
self-organized
based on that data.

00:08:10.211 --> 00:08:11.790
It's almost a roadmap for

00:08:11.791 --> 00:08:13.780
where our field is
going as a whole,

00:08:13.781 --> 00:08:16.600
the way it positions itself
relative to machine learning.

00:08:16.601 --> 00:08:18.585
So, we have a weighty obligation,

00:08:18.586 --> 00:08:21.685
panelists to chart
the course here.

00:08:21.686 --> 00:08:23.755
I'd like to begin by

00:08:23.756 --> 00:08:25.800
thinking about the frontiers
by looking back.

00:08:25.801 --> 00:08:28.760
So, I'd like to begin
by asking each of you,

00:08:28.761 --> 00:08:30.290
with respect to your own work,

00:08:30.291 --> 00:08:32.320
your own disciplinary
perspective,

00:08:32.321 --> 00:08:34.450
to think about where you were

00:08:34.451 --> 00:08:36.775
10 years ago with respect
to machine learning,

00:08:36.776 --> 00:08:38.305
and what has happened in

00:08:38.306 --> 00:08:41.199
the last 10 years that you
found personally surprising,

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:42.215
from the perspective of

00:08:42.216 --> 00:08:43.955
breakthroughs in
machine learning.

00:08:43.956 --> 00:08:46.295
&gt;&gt; Yes. I mean,

00:08:46.296 --> 00:08:49.040
I can name quite a few
like 10 years ago,

00:08:49.041 --> 00:08:52.470
I was barely at the
time of graduation.

00:08:52.471 --> 00:08:54.010
It was also, in terms of

00:08:54.011 --> 00:08:57.100
the overall conditions,
there was recession,

00:08:57.101 --> 00:09:00.400
there were hardly any good jobs

00:09:00.401 --> 00:09:03.035
in computer science and
that's what it seemed like.

00:09:03.036 --> 00:09:05.200
So, I was so lucky to get

00:09:05.201 --> 00:09:08.945
an academic job that taught
a new seer that, that year,

00:09:08.946 --> 00:09:12.169
and now a decade later,

00:09:12.170 --> 00:09:14.660
the opportunities for people

00:09:14.661 --> 00:09:16.660
in machine learning are
just, seem endless.

00:09:16.661 --> 00:09:20.045
There's so many great
positions in industry.

00:09:20.046 --> 00:09:23.365
Every university wants to hire
more in machine learning.

00:09:23.366 --> 00:09:25.965
So, in terms of opportunities,

00:09:25.966 --> 00:09:27.420
it seems a lot.

00:09:27.421 --> 00:09:28.590
&gt;&gt; So, you find the prospects for

00:09:28.591 --> 00:09:32.020
employment in machine learning
is personal surprising?

00:09:33.260 --> 00:09:38.120
&gt;&gt; It's not surprising but
there's been a very big shift,

00:09:38.121 --> 00:09:41.440
and it has happened much
sooner than I thought,

00:09:41.441 --> 00:09:43.945
and there has indeed
been progress,

00:09:43.946 --> 00:09:45.970
what we thought in terms of,

00:09:45.971 --> 00:09:48.000
can we train these
neural networks

00:09:48.001 --> 00:09:49.740
at that scale and really

00:09:49.741 --> 00:09:53.600
get up to these levels of
performance in practice?

00:09:53.601 --> 00:09:56.060
That has been surprising
to everybody.

00:09:56.061 --> 00:09:58.030
That's where I would like to been

00:09:58.031 --> 00:10:00.755
on both extreme ends
of the spectrum,

00:10:00.756 --> 00:10:03.610
I've done theory,
really thought deeply

00:10:03.611 --> 00:10:07.120
about what machine learning
problems are hard?

00:10:07.121 --> 00:10:09.650
What is the complexity
of these problems?

00:10:09.651 --> 00:10:12.100
Being at Amazon and
know at N-Media,

00:10:12.101 --> 00:10:14.080
I see extremely practical things

00:10:14.081 --> 00:10:17.100
where we have to launch
a product very soon,

00:10:17.101 --> 00:10:18.909
we have lots of
other constraints,

00:10:18.910 --> 00:10:21.790
we have customers
having requirements.

00:10:21.791 --> 00:10:25.795
So, you'll have to make due
with what's currently there,

00:10:25.796 --> 00:10:27.740
you can't always think

00:10:27.741 --> 00:10:30.425
about pushing the frontiers
in the next say,

00:10:30.426 --> 00:10:31.780
five years or so.

00:10:31.781 --> 00:10:33.340
So, being on the two sides,

00:10:33.341 --> 00:10:34.830
what I've seen as we need

00:10:34.831 --> 00:10:37.660
more research in
the middle ground.

00:10:37.661 --> 00:10:40.030
I felt that in a way,

00:10:40.031 --> 00:10:41.760
some of the theory was holding us

00:10:41.761 --> 00:10:44.430
back because there was
a lot of pessimism.

00:10:44.431 --> 00:10:45.920
There was even a paper in

00:10:45.921 --> 00:10:48.200
my own field of tensors
with the title,

00:10:48.201 --> 00:10:50.720
All Tensor Problems are NP-hard.

00:10:50.721 --> 00:10:53.680
So, whenever I would
tell people about

00:10:53.681 --> 00:10:55.610
my tensor algorithm and

00:10:55.611 --> 00:10:58.890
our tensor method that we
were very excited about,

00:10:58.891 --> 00:11:00.180
people would be
like, "But I thought

00:11:00.181 --> 00:11:02.060
these are NP-hard problems. "

00:11:02.061 --> 00:11:05.250
So, and it was a similar thing
with neural networks.

00:11:05.251 --> 00:11:08.740
A lot of empirical researchers
for decades felt like they

00:11:08.741 --> 00:11:10.470
were pulled away from it

00:11:10.471 --> 00:11:13.845
because many people
thought it was impossible.

00:11:13.846 --> 00:11:16.800
So, I have a lot of
respect for practitioners

00:11:16.801 --> 00:11:19.180
and the early engineers
who really said,

00:11:19.181 --> 00:11:21.295
"Let's just make it work.

00:11:21.296 --> 00:11:23.915
Maybe it looks hacky,
maybe it's not.

00:11:23.916 --> 00:11:26.070
It doesn't seem to follow

00:11:26.071 --> 00:11:29.230
the principles but just let's
see how to make it work."

00:11:29.231 --> 00:11:32.290
So, that's a great
starting point.

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:36.725
I have a lot of respect for
that and now the question is,

00:11:36.726 --> 00:11:40.545
how do we nurture that
into a much bigger thing?

00:11:40.546 --> 00:11:43.909
That's where we need
research on robustness,

00:11:43.910 --> 00:11:47.025
on how to do it with less data,

00:11:47.026 --> 00:11:50.020
how to do it in a lot
of different domains,

00:11:50.021 --> 00:11:51.700
how to scale up the problem.

00:11:51.701 --> 00:11:54.025
&gt;&gt; Let's not do the whole panel
in one question here.

00:11:54.026 --> 00:11:55.180
&gt;&gt; Sorry.

00:11:55.181 --> 00:11:56.620
&gt;&gt; We want to thank you.

00:11:56.621 --> 00:11:58.180
Looking back last 10 years.

00:11:58.181 --> 00:11:58.790
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:11:58.791 --> 00:12:01.270
&gt;&gt; As concretely as possible,
what's surprised you?

00:12:01.271 --> 00:12:02.200
&gt;&gt; Go ahead.

00:12:02.201 --> 00:12:03.850
&gt;&gt; So, 10 years ago,

00:12:03.851 --> 00:12:06.330
I was starting my second year
of graduate school.

00:12:06.331 --> 00:12:08.160
I was spending
about half of my time

00:12:08.161 --> 00:12:09.770
working on legitimate research,

00:12:09.771 --> 00:12:11.080
and half of it working on

00:12:11.081 --> 00:12:12.770
this Netflix prize problem

00:12:12.771 --> 00:12:14.520
that Netflix had
recently come up with,

00:12:14.521 --> 00:12:15.580
it was to solve

00:12:15.581 --> 00:12:17.700
their recommendation problem
and they would say,

00:12:17.701 --> 00:12:18.990
"We would give you a million
dollars if you do that,

00:12:18.991 --> 00:12:22.380
" so that was appealing
to a graduate student.

00:12:22.381 --> 00:12:25.850
So, I remember thinking

00:12:25.851 --> 00:12:28.825
just a year prior
when I was saying,

00:12:28.826 --> 00:12:30.740
"Where do you go in private
school or what to do?"

00:12:30.741 --> 00:12:32.160
I had flirted a bit with

00:12:32.161 --> 00:12:33.480
a lot of this multi
computer science.

00:12:33.481 --> 00:12:35.485
So, I spent sometime

00:12:35.486 --> 00:12:36.700
researching
programming languages,

00:12:36.701 --> 00:12:38.125
sometime researching theory,

00:12:38.126 --> 00:12:39.880
and those both seemed
interesting to me

00:12:39.881 --> 00:12:41.530
but nothing seemed to
be quite the right fit

00:12:41.531 --> 00:12:43.400
and when I encountered
machine learning

00:12:43.401 --> 00:12:45.050
actually via this Netflix prize,

00:12:45.051 --> 00:12:46.435
this problem of, I see

00:12:46.436 --> 00:12:48.240
what ratings you've given
movies in the past,

00:12:48.241 --> 00:12:49.680
how can I predict to
what you're going

00:12:49.681 --> 00:12:51.520
to rate a movie in the future.

00:12:51.521 --> 00:12:53.130
I thought, " This is
perfect and this is

00:12:53.131 --> 00:12:55.395
the future because
it combines people,

00:12:55.396 --> 00:12:57.995
information for people about
the data they express,

00:12:57.996 --> 00:13:00.010
It combines in
mathematical modeling,

00:13:00.011 --> 00:13:02.565
give statistics and algorithms,"

00:13:02.566 --> 00:13:04.920
all the best parts of
computer science and math,

00:13:04.921 --> 00:13:06.325
and people to me.

00:13:06.326 --> 00:13:09.090
I was just shocked by
the idea that, wow,

00:13:09.091 --> 00:13:10.820
we can learn so much
about people from

00:13:10.821 --> 00:13:13.090
their data and it want
us to explore that more,

00:13:13.091 --> 00:13:15.460
and that's what got me
into that competition

00:13:15.461 --> 00:13:17.290
but also into machine
learning to begin with.

00:13:17.291 --> 00:13:18.160
So, this is not really an answer

00:13:18.161 --> 00:13:19.255
to your question
because I would think,

00:13:19.256 --> 00:13:20.370
I'm not at all surprised

00:13:20.371 --> 00:13:21.460
about what's happened
to machine learning,

00:13:21.461 --> 00:13:22.390
it seemed to me at that point

00:13:22.391 --> 00:13:23.560
that this would be the future

00:13:23.561 --> 00:13:25.720
that everyone should want
to be in this field.

00:13:25.721 --> 00:13:26.965
It just seemed incredible,

00:13:26.966 --> 00:13:28.870
and it seemed it really
had a lot of potential

00:13:28.871 --> 00:13:32.555
both taking theory and
translating into practice,

00:13:32.556 --> 00:13:35.775
and I think we're seeing
more and more of that.

00:13:35.776 --> 00:13:38.920
&gt;&gt; Yes. So, one thing
that I think about,

00:13:38.921 --> 00:13:42.640
so 10 years ago, I was in high
school now that's a joke.

00:13:43.160 --> 00:13:46.490
But almost, it's close,

00:13:46.491 --> 00:13:48.830
plus or minus five years.

00:13:49.210 --> 00:13:51.800
So, if you think
about the machine

00:13:51.801 --> 00:13:53.640
learning papers that
were accepted to

00:13:53.641 --> 00:13:55.440
NIPS or top machine

00:13:55.441 --> 00:13:57.125
learning conferences in
general, 10 years ago,

00:13:57.126 --> 00:14:00.260
you would see a lot
of them were well,

00:14:00.261 --> 00:14:01.735
at least in probabilistic
machine learning where

00:14:01.736 --> 00:14:04.835
a graphical model put
distributions over things,

00:14:04.836 --> 00:14:08.090
and then three
pages of gradients.

00:14:08.091 --> 00:14:11.910
How do you learn the thing?
That was people got in,

00:14:11.911 --> 00:14:13.260
got interested
probabilistic programming,

00:14:13.261 --> 00:14:14.700
so a lot of that got automated.

00:14:14.701 --> 00:14:16.405
But if you think about it,

00:14:16.406 --> 00:14:17.960
in the course of 10 years,

00:14:17.961 --> 00:14:20.945
we eliminated via
various techniques,

00:14:20.946 --> 00:14:23.050
a bunch of those pages of papers.

00:14:23.051 --> 00:14:25.310
So now, people will
take them for granted.

00:14:25.311 --> 00:14:29.515
So, there was a very clear
process of make it work,

00:14:29.516 --> 00:14:33.820
make it fast, and then
make it very easy to use.

00:14:33.821 --> 00:14:37.590
If you look at the papers
that are submitted twice,

00:14:37.591 --> 00:14:40.350
CLR, HCML, these days,

00:14:40.351 --> 00:14:43.510
the build on this giant stack of

00:14:43.511 --> 00:14:48.370
pretty solid, automated
complex work.

00:14:48.371 --> 00:14:50.410
There are all these
probabilistic programming

00:14:50.411 --> 00:14:52.315
libraries that you can use,

00:14:52.316 --> 00:14:53.640
and you can just build more

00:14:53.641 --> 00:14:55.820
complex constructs and concepts,

00:14:55.821 --> 00:14:57.965
and models in
a fraction of the time.

00:14:57.966 --> 00:15:01.060
I think that's paying
dividends for the field.

00:15:01.061 --> 00:15:02.210
So, we are getting through

00:15:02.211 --> 00:15:05.465
these crazy exponential
growth phase,

00:15:05.466 --> 00:15:09.250
in terms of I feel
interesting measurable ideas,

00:15:09.251 --> 00:15:11.240
that wouldn't have been
possible if we're still

00:15:11.241 --> 00:15:13.725
all deriving gradients
for our beta model.

00:15:13.726 --> 00:15:15.370
So, the first one I tried,

00:15:15.371 --> 00:15:17.520
the first time I used
Theano which was coming

00:15:17.521 --> 00:15:20.240
out I think from MILA maybe,

00:15:20.241 --> 00:15:23.570
Theano was this library
that you would specify

00:15:23.571 --> 00:15:27.180
variables and it would do
the gradients for you,

00:15:27.181 --> 00:15:30.690
you could just specify
any model pretty much.

00:15:30.691 --> 00:15:31.730
But it was slow,

00:15:31.731 --> 00:15:32.780
at the first time I tried it

00:15:32.781 --> 00:15:34.010
and then I tried it a year after,

00:15:34.011 --> 00:15:36.125
it was faster, and now we have

00:15:36.126 --> 00:15:39.720
crazy toolkits to do this work.

00:15:40.390 --> 00:15:43.030
&gt;&gt; All right. Do you
want to speak to this?

00:15:43.031 --> 00:15:44.650
&gt;&gt; Yes. So, 10 years ago,

00:15:44.651 --> 00:15:48.415
we started to work on trying to,

00:15:48.416 --> 00:15:50.275
essentially a financial variable,

00:15:50.276 --> 00:15:51.760
a Russian model, where you

00:15:51.761 --> 00:15:53.200
use machine learning tool that is

00:15:53.201 --> 00:15:58.285
[inaudible] to proxy
the optimum instrument.

00:15:58.286 --> 00:16:03.430
So, not many people know
the context here but yeah.

00:16:03.431 --> 00:16:06.130
So, I think the Russian
is one of the main tools

00:16:06.131 --> 00:16:09.310
[inaudible] metrics that
deals with pneumatic,

00:16:09.311 --> 00:16:12.190
if you want to estimate
the demand or demand curve,

00:16:12.191 --> 00:16:14.305
you need instruments to

00:16:14.306 --> 00:16:16.180
shape the supply for
our alternative in

00:16:16.181 --> 00:16:18.280
trade [inaudible] the moves of

00:16:18.281 --> 00:16:20.425
the equilibrium quantity
along the demand curve.

00:16:20.426 --> 00:16:22.960
So, we really need a good
instrument on machine learning,

00:16:22.961 --> 00:16:26.330
to do a good instrument.

00:16:26.331 --> 00:16:28.950
What was actually
fascinating at that time,

00:16:28.951 --> 00:16:31.485
although many machine learning
tools without any theory.

00:16:31.486 --> 00:16:32.790
If you press a button

00:16:32.791 --> 00:16:34.140
would it work if
there is no theory?

00:16:34.141 --> 00:16:37.620
This seemed to work amazingly
well, hard to imagine.

00:16:37.621 --> 00:16:38.220
&gt;&gt; It will.

00:16:38.221 --> 00:16:41.370
&gt;&gt; So, I saw that to do

00:16:41.371 --> 00:16:44.275
some theoretical work
or theory around it,

00:16:44.276 --> 00:16:49.515
focusing on how to find
the problems. [inaudible]

00:16:49.516 --> 00:16:52.940
&gt;&gt; All right. So, still
looking 10 years back.

00:16:52.941 --> 00:16:56.400
Let's now talk about what
you've thought would

00:16:56.401 --> 00:16:58.910
be working by now

00:16:58.911 --> 00:17:00.330
that turns out to be

00:17:00.331 --> 00:17:02.700
a disappointing failure
as you see in marketing?

00:17:02.701 --> 00:17:04.920
What in retrospect has

00:17:04.921 --> 00:17:06.510
turned out to be
much harder than you

00:17:06.511 --> 00:17:11.080
would though it would be?

00:17:11.081 --> 00:17:15.050
&gt;&gt; I thought an answer and
then I thought, "Maybe not".

00:17:15.360 --> 00:17:18.145
&gt;&gt; But here you go.

00:17:18.146 --> 00:17:21.890
&gt;&gt; All right. Please
don't throw stuff at me.

00:17:21.891 --> 00:17:26.480
Reinforcement learning. No, okay.

00:17:26.481 --> 00:17:27.895
So, I think when I

00:17:27.896 --> 00:17:30.240
first started looking into
reinforcement learning,

00:17:30.241 --> 00:17:32.285
I thought, we are,

00:17:32.286 --> 00:17:34.330
almost it's one of
those times where you think,

00:17:34.331 --> 00:17:35.800
I'm out about basically
out of a job.

00:17:35.801 --> 00:17:38.165
People are figuring
out, as we speak,

00:17:38.166 --> 00:17:40.390
and so by the time

00:17:40.391 --> 00:17:43.690
I end up contributing something,
it's going to be sold.

00:17:43.691 --> 00:17:45.335
That hasn't been the case.

00:17:45.336 --> 00:17:48.620
So, in some sense, people are
making a lot of progress,

00:17:48.621 --> 00:17:51.235
but there's still
a lot of work to do,

00:17:51.236 --> 00:17:53.655
and in some sense, it's
getting interesting now.

00:17:53.656 --> 00:17:56.340
&gt;&gt; I remember being at
a NIPS workshop on the frontiers

00:17:56.341 --> 00:17:58.700
of reinforcement learning
15 years ago,

00:17:58.701 --> 00:18:01.485
that was everybody been moaning
the fact that there was

00:18:01.486 --> 00:18:03.160
almost nothing realistic
that you could

00:18:03.161 --> 00:18:05.060
do with reinforcement learning.

00:18:05.061 --> 00:18:06.160
That certainly has changed.

00:18:06.161 --> 00:18:07.470
The reinforcement learning
has come a long way.

00:18:07.471 --> 00:18:08.960
&gt;&gt; We really nailed
the cart pole.

00:18:08.961 --> 00:18:11.025
&gt;&gt; A lot of games. That's all.

00:18:11.026 --> 00:18:13.200
No. I mean, I do think,

00:18:13.201 --> 00:18:15.360
there's still realistic
applications.

00:18:15.361 --> 00:18:17.890
There have been
very few real use cases

00:18:17.891 --> 00:18:19.505
of deep reinforcement learning,

00:18:19.506 --> 00:18:21.405
especially the ones that scale.

00:18:21.406 --> 00:18:25.020
So, I mean it's

00:18:25.021 --> 00:18:27.780
perfectly fine to say we
can solve games that are

00:18:27.781 --> 00:18:30.860
hard for humans by
training on billions of

00:18:30.861 --> 00:18:34.465
examples but it's a very
different ball game to ask.

00:18:34.466 --> 00:18:36.030
How would we solve robotics?

00:18:36.031 --> 00:18:38.490
How would we automate
manufacturing?

00:18:38.491 --> 00:18:41.480
How would we really do

00:18:41.481 --> 00:18:44.615
automated machine learning
at the end of the day.

00:18:44.616 --> 00:18:47.575
The holy grail is ultimately,

00:18:47.576 --> 00:18:49.720
they reborn backyards
in their homes,

00:18:49.721 --> 00:18:53.430
people without even
thinking keep training and

00:18:53.431 --> 00:18:56.680
personalizing
their different AI tools

00:18:56.681 --> 00:18:58.340
and frameworks and say,

00:18:58.341 --> 00:19:00.525
their personal devices,
in their home,

00:19:00.526 --> 00:19:04.545
or at work, and
that's a long way.

00:19:04.546 --> 00:19:06.680
In light right now, I mean,

00:19:06.681 --> 00:19:10.260
it has become a lot easier
than what it was 10 years ago.

00:19:10.261 --> 00:19:13.485
The software frameworks
have gotten amazingly good.

00:19:13.486 --> 00:19:17.220
The computational scale has
been growing every day.

00:19:17.221 --> 00:19:20.605
We're even close to breaking
the exascale barrier.

00:19:20.606 --> 00:19:22.500
So, the progress there has

00:19:22.501 --> 00:19:24.545
been amazing, but there is still,

00:19:24.546 --> 00:19:26.780
I think algorithmic
progress to be

00:19:26.781 --> 00:19:29.355
done to ask how we
can exploit that,

00:19:29.356 --> 00:19:31.130
to make it more seamless,

00:19:31.131 --> 00:19:34.445
to go from raw data to answers,

00:19:34.446 --> 00:19:38.945
to a fully working AI system
with almost no intervention.

00:19:38.946 --> 00:19:41.635
We are so far from
that dream today.

00:19:41.636 --> 00:19:46.880
&gt;&gt; So, what other really
concrete technologies,

00:19:46.881 --> 00:19:48.990
working use cases would
you have expected

00:19:48.991 --> 00:19:50.630
10 years ago that
you're surprised

00:19:50.631 --> 00:19:52.130
not to have seen
progress on today?

00:19:52.131 --> 00:19:55.610
&gt;&gt; In this is a case where
we've had a lot of progress,

00:19:55.611 --> 00:19:59.710
but I'm surprised that we
haven't solved these problems.

00:19:59.711 --> 00:20:01.775
So, I think there are lots
of opportunities for using

00:20:01.776 --> 00:20:04.420
machine learning very
profitably in healthcare,

00:20:04.421 --> 00:20:06.550
but for the large part,

00:20:06.551 --> 00:20:07.310
a lot of problems that

00:20:07.311 --> 00:20:08.485
are essentially
prediction problem,

00:20:08.486 --> 00:20:10.700
which is like bread and
butter machine learning.

00:20:10.701 --> 00:20:12.185
Supervised learning
problems classified as

00:20:12.186 --> 00:20:13.790
patient as someone's going to

00:20:13.791 --> 00:20:15.570
respond or not stratified

00:20:15.571 --> 00:20:17.070
this patient in
the following way,

00:20:17.071 --> 00:20:20.610
but the major barrier is
actually data collection.

00:20:20.611 --> 00:20:22.090
We have small dataset

00:20:22.091 --> 00:20:23.795
of patients that are
heavily guarded.

00:20:23.796 --> 00:20:24.680
We don't have any good way of

00:20:24.681 --> 00:20:26.535
aggregating them across centers,

00:20:26.536 --> 00:20:28.130
different people with
different features

00:20:28.131 --> 00:20:29.555
but no standardization.

00:20:29.556 --> 00:20:30.830
That's what put them
on those principles of

00:20:30.831 --> 00:20:32.150
barriers to solving
a lot of these problems,

00:20:32.151 --> 00:20:34.085
making a lot of headway.

00:20:34.086 --> 00:20:35.650
In the cases that I think
when we had had a big

00:20:35.651 --> 00:20:37.780
successes are when
institutions spend

00:20:37.781 --> 00:20:39.510
a lot of time and effort
pulling their data

00:20:39.511 --> 00:20:42.255
together to get a data
better solutions.

00:20:42.256 --> 00:20:44.145
So, I found it surprising.

00:20:44.146 --> 00:20:45.740
&gt;&gt; Cool. By the way, Lester,

00:20:45.741 --> 00:20:48.860
if you hold your mike a
little lower down. Yes.

00:20:49.700 --> 00:20:52.315
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] to
your right. Yes.

00:20:52.316 --> 00:20:54.445
&gt;&gt; Well, let Jeff
help you out then.

00:20:54.446 --> 00:20:55.640
So do you wanna
speak [inaudible].

00:20:55.641 --> 00:20:59.410
Oh, there we go.

00:20:59.411 --> 00:21:02.270
You've got upgraded. Okay?

00:21:05.460 --> 00:21:09.655
&gt;&gt; Now, I'm off. I'm good?

00:21:09.656 --> 00:21:13.920
Alright, it's back on?
Apple, can you hear me?

00:21:13.921 --> 00:21:14.460
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:21:14.461 --> 00:21:17.685
&gt;&gt; Good. It's very important.

00:21:17.686 --> 00:21:20.380
So, talking about health care,

00:21:20.381 --> 00:21:22.890
one thing that comes to mind is,

00:21:22.891 --> 00:21:25.350
so working in Combion,

00:21:25.351 --> 00:21:26.940
the intersectional
Combion medicine,

00:21:26.941 --> 00:21:29.610
what I've seen is, describing

00:21:29.611 --> 00:21:31.050
your uncertainty over
your predictions

00:21:31.051 --> 00:21:32.110
is super important.

00:21:32.111 --> 00:21:36.500
Yet in healthcare applications

00:21:36.501 --> 00:21:38.500
particularly, split
in two fields,

00:21:38.501 --> 00:21:40.680
either poor predictors
that give you

00:21:40.681 --> 00:21:41.700
uncertainty estimates that are

00:21:41.701 --> 00:21:43.955
calibrated and they're useful,

00:21:43.956 --> 00:21:46.165
but then they're so wide.

00:21:46.166 --> 00:21:49.260
They are very really actionable,

00:21:49.261 --> 00:21:51.960
or you got these very
good predictors,

00:21:51.961 --> 00:21:53.825
but then we cannot estimate nice

00:21:53.826 --> 00:21:56.430
calibrated uncertainty
estimates from them.

00:21:56.431 --> 00:21:58.970
So, that's a big challenge

00:21:58.971 --> 00:22:00.780
for us because in
some sense the data doesn't

00:22:00.781 --> 00:22:02.470
come until people see

00:22:02.471 --> 00:22:06.135
the usefulness,
clinicians in particular.

00:22:06.136 --> 00:22:09.100
It was, when I was in the UK,

00:22:09.101 --> 00:22:11.955
I was sitting in
this meeting where there was

00:22:11.956 --> 00:22:14.180
the Health Care Regulatory

00:22:14.181 --> 00:22:15.780
Agency and they we're thinking of

00:22:15.781 --> 00:22:20.110
using machinery and
that was in 2013, 2012.

00:22:20.111 --> 00:22:21.700
They were not thinking of
using machine learning,

00:22:21.701 --> 00:22:23.610
but I couldn't, they
were like, "Okay,

00:22:23.611 --> 00:22:25.180
so we know we can use
machine learning,

00:22:25.181 --> 00:22:27.650
but how do we know it's useful?".

00:22:27.651 --> 00:22:29.120
Because always you can develop

00:22:29.121 --> 00:22:30.895
personalized interventions
and we're like,

00:22:30.896 --> 00:22:33.560
"We already do. We
look at the patients".

00:22:34.260 --> 00:22:36.480
But instead nobody's are new med,

00:22:36.481 --> 00:22:39.330
these are informed by
data and they say,

00:22:39.331 --> 00:22:40.955
"Well, we are informed by data

00:22:40.956 --> 00:22:42.380
where we have case studies".

00:22:42.381 --> 00:22:44.395
So, it was a hard sell

00:22:44.396 --> 00:22:46.840
and until we show that there
are these predictors give

00:22:46.841 --> 00:22:49.960
you nice predictions
that you can throw

00:22:49.961 --> 00:22:53.440
