the entire agent

00:34:24.731 --> 00:34:27.760
collapse at the end. [inaudible]

00:34:27.761 --> 00:34:31.990
&gt;&gt; So, objective achieved.

00:34:31.991 --> 00:34:34.990
It's like the Reward 100.

00:34:34.991 --> 00:34:39.055
So, then they start constraining
the length of the legs,

00:34:39.056 --> 00:34:40.600
and now it does skipping,

00:34:40.601 --> 00:34:42.200
but this is the problem, right?

00:34:42.201 --> 00:34:43.770
If you just let it go on

00:34:43.771 --> 00:34:45.485
an objective function
without thinking,

00:34:45.486 --> 00:34:48.810
it will just find shortcuts.

00:34:48.811 --> 00:34:51.850
There was a thing like
were there was a rocket.

00:34:51.851 --> 00:34:53.010
Another one of

00:34:53.011 --> 00:34:54.390
those tight fragments
problems was designing

00:34:54.391 --> 00:34:57.825
a rocket that goes up and then
lands in another location.

00:34:57.826 --> 00:34:59.280
So, what the system

00:34:59.281 --> 00:35:00.810
found without constraints
from physics,

00:35:00.811 --> 00:35:02.730
was if you go underground,

00:35:02.731 --> 00:35:05.660
it's actually
consumes less energy.

00:35:05.790 --> 00:35:08.120
So, it actually
designed the rocket to

00:35:08.121 --> 00:35:10.830
just point down and just dig.

00:35:11.640 --> 00:35:16.090
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, what should
we take from this?

00:35:18.650 --> 00:35:20.870
&gt;&gt; A lot of my work now is about

00:35:20.871 --> 00:35:23.780
automating and automated
Machine Learning.

00:35:25.070 --> 00:35:29.510
As much as I want that kind
of result to be achieved,

00:35:29.511 --> 00:35:31.470
I think the human
will still need to be

00:35:31.471 --> 00:35:35.670
the entity that decides
what you're learning,

00:35:35.671 --> 00:35:38.225
directing the learning
towards something.

00:35:38.226 --> 00:35:41.590
And so, that's I think
something that we'll

00:35:41.591 --> 00:35:44.500
never probably automate
or solve completely.

00:35:44.501 --> 00:35:48.870
Because especially if you specify

00:35:48.871 --> 00:35:53.795
an objective the system will
do anything to achieve it.

00:35:53.796 --> 00:35:55.690
And now, you start specifying

00:35:55.691 --> 00:35:57.530
too many constraints and then
you have other problems.

00:35:57.531 --> 00:36:00.100
So, that's a big problem that I

00:36:00.101 --> 00:36:01.390
may be due to lack of

00:36:01.391 --> 00:36:03.870
imagination cannot see
how it'll be solved.

00:36:03.871 --> 00:36:05.545
&gt;&gt; And to add to that,

00:36:05.546 --> 00:36:07.880
I think even some of
the critical failures

00:36:07.881 --> 00:36:10.175
that'll come about and
that have come about

00:36:10.176 --> 00:36:12.560
will be more human failures

00:36:12.561 --> 00:36:15.170
than the machine-learning or AI.

00:36:15.171 --> 00:36:17.040
In the media, it'll
be easy to say,

00:36:17.041 --> 00:36:18.680
"Oh, look AI failed."

00:36:18.681 --> 00:36:21.040
The famous one is
the Uber accident,

00:36:21.041 --> 00:36:22.325
of the you know where.

00:36:22.326 --> 00:36:25.220
The pedestrian was actually
detected by the sensors,

00:36:25.221 --> 00:36:27.550
so it was not
a technological failure.

00:36:27.551 --> 00:36:30.810
But it was a failure
in terms of how

00:36:30.811 --> 00:36:34.320
the processing did not
reach the end decision.

00:36:34.321 --> 00:36:38.195
Right? And so, if the system
was extensively tested,

00:36:38.196 --> 00:36:40.140
it would have been
definitely caught,

00:36:40.141 --> 00:36:42.570
because there is,
we all know about

00:36:42.571 --> 00:36:45.310
false alarm and detection curve.

00:36:45.311 --> 00:36:48.310
So, we can ask where are
we in that operating?

00:36:48.311 --> 00:36:50.590
What is the operating point
in that curve?

00:36:50.591 --> 00:36:54.070
And then make a
decision based on that.

00:36:54.071 --> 00:36:56.110
So, in this case,

00:36:56.111 --> 00:36:57.640
either it slipped through

00:36:57.641 --> 00:37:01.005
or it was decided
that we would just

00:37:01.006 --> 00:37:03.655
have this human driver
who would just

00:37:03.656 --> 00:37:07.175
take care of any failure
of Machine Learning.

00:37:07.176 --> 00:37:09.050
But that's again, not taking into

00:37:09.051 --> 00:37:10.920
account the human attention span.

00:37:10.921 --> 00:37:14.220
So, if they're continuously
driving forever

00:37:14.221 --> 00:37:17.620
and the chances of
failure are tiny,

00:37:17.621 --> 00:37:19.940
but when they happen
they're catastrophic.

00:37:19.941 --> 00:37:23.110
So, I think we'll
still face a lot of

00:37:23.111 --> 00:37:27.075
challenges in
many such domains where

00:37:27.076 --> 00:37:30.300
AI system will be in
the loop with the critical

00:37:30.301 --> 00:37:32.540
decision-making and even having

00:37:32.541 --> 00:37:35.950
human or many humans may
not be the solution.

00:37:35.951 --> 00:37:37.660
&gt;&gt; Well, so, Niccolo and Animah,

00:37:37.661 --> 00:37:39.990
both in your last
responses raised

00:37:39.991 --> 00:37:42.360
this idea of how people
were going to be

00:37:42.361 --> 00:37:45.330
reacting in real time to deployed

00:37:45.331 --> 00:37:47.510
ML systems and I think
this really touches

00:37:47.511 --> 00:37:50.150
on this question of
the limits of ML.

00:37:50.151 --> 00:37:51.900
It seems like right now,

00:37:51.901 --> 00:37:55.200
we haven't gotten very good
socially at understanding

00:37:55.201 --> 00:37:56.689
these limits and understanding

00:37:56.690 --> 00:37:58.910
the boundaries of where
these systems break down,

00:37:58.911 --> 00:38:00.815
how we ought to
interact with them.

00:38:00.816 --> 00:38:02.370
Do you guys think
this is something

00:38:02.371 --> 00:38:03.795
that we're going
to get better at?

00:38:03.796 --> 00:38:05.730
Do you think the systems
are going to have to

00:38:05.731 --> 00:38:08.305
adapt in a way to make it
easier for us to do this?

00:38:08.306 --> 00:38:09.760
If so, do you have

00:38:09.761 --> 00:38:12.090
any thoughts on what
that might look like?

00:38:12.091 --> 00:38:13.430
Are things going to get worse as

00:38:13.431 --> 00:38:16.120
these systems get
more widely deployed?

00:38:16.600 --> 00:38:21.500
&gt;&gt; Possibly, yeah. There are

00:38:21.501 --> 00:38:23.860
people working on it,
but there is still,

00:38:23.861 --> 00:38:26.070
compared to the size
of the problem and

00:38:26.071 --> 00:38:28.175
the size of the Machine
Learning field,

00:38:28.176 --> 00:38:31.310
there is a very narrow
contact surface in between,

00:38:31.311 --> 00:38:34.240
which is HCI work,
Human-Computer Interaction.

00:38:34.241 --> 00:38:36.070
So, things such as

00:38:36.071 --> 00:38:38.945
understanding and
visualizing uncertainty,

00:38:38.946 --> 00:38:42.140
providing it as a feedback to

00:38:42.141 --> 00:38:44.945
the to the human operator or
the human making decision.

00:38:44.946 --> 00:38:47.730
It's still something
that is poorly

00:38:47.731 --> 00:38:50.870
understood and to be developed.

00:38:50.871 --> 00:38:52.195
There is some there is some work,

00:38:52.196 --> 00:38:54.255
but it's very specific.

00:38:54.256 --> 00:38:56.390
Last night at dinner
I was talking about,

00:38:56.391 --> 00:38:57.530
there is an app called Dark

00:38:57.531 --> 00:39:00.750
Sky that does nowcasting
of the weather,

00:39:00.751 --> 00:39:01.970
which is very useful in Boston

00:39:01.971 --> 00:39:03.520
because you don't
want to get soaked.

00:39:03.521 --> 00:39:05.650
And it predicts basically

00:39:05.651 --> 00:39:07.525
a time series of rain intensity.

00:39:07.526 --> 00:39:09.260
And uncertainty estimate is

00:39:09.261 --> 00:39:10.915
super important because people,

00:39:10.916 --> 00:39:12.300
and there is
a whole book about it

00:39:12.301 --> 00:39:14.775
which actually are
social media collective,

00:39:14.776 --> 00:39:16.860
which was about following

00:39:16.861 --> 00:39:19.180
the National Weather Service and

00:39:19.181 --> 00:39:20.210
studying how they think

00:39:20.211 --> 00:39:21.900
about uncertainty
and predictions,

00:39:21.901 --> 00:39:24.190
it's called Masters of
Uncertainty.

00:39:24.700 --> 00:39:27.365
So, for weather prediction,

00:39:27.366 --> 00:39:30.160
conveying uncertainty about
your prediction is super

00:39:30.161 --> 00:39:33.490
important and this time series,
the way the app does it,

00:39:33.491 --> 00:39:37.910
it shows basically
a smooth Gaussian process

00:39:37.911 --> 00:39:39.950
looking line that shows

00:39:39.951 --> 00:39:42.315
the boundaries of
the uncertainty.

00:39:42.316 --> 00:39:44.820
And stuff like that is
absolutely brilliant,

00:39:44.821 --> 00:39:47.715
because it allows you to
understand what's going on,

00:39:47.716 --> 00:39:49.905
but it's very narrowly focused on

00:39:49.906 --> 00:39:52.610
some domains right now and we
need more contact surface.

00:39:52.611 --> 00:39:53.780
&gt;&gt; But it seems like it allows

00:39:53.781 --> 00:39:54.790
you to know what's going on if

00:39:54.791 --> 00:39:57.025
you're already very good at
thinking about uncertainty.

00:39:57.026 --> 00:40:00.170
It seems like, but the
broad population has

00:40:00.171 --> 00:40:04.155
a lot of trouble thinking about
probabilistic statements.

00:40:04.156 --> 00:40:05.700
Are we going to get better as a

00:40:05.701 --> 00:40:07.040
society because we
are going to be so

00:40:07.041 --> 00:40:08.370
surrounded by these things or are

00:40:08.371 --> 00:40:10.470
the systems going
to have to adapt?

00:40:10.471 --> 00:40:14.590
&gt;&gt; I think we will need to
get more educated about it.

00:40:14.591 --> 00:40:16.050
So, for instance,
for the elections,

00:40:16.051 --> 00:40:17.980
the New York Times
is another version

00:40:17.981 --> 00:40:19.790
of these uncertainty
estimates things,

00:40:19.791 --> 00:40:21.320
where you have a dial that goes

00:40:21.321 --> 00:40:23.190
Republican or Democrat and they

00:40:23.191 --> 00:40:27.700
added like a jitter to show
the uncertainty around.

00:40:27.780 --> 00:40:30.100
Exactly, the jitter,
in the beginning,

00:40:30.101 --> 00:40:36.040
was terrifying and
then it turned out,

00:40:36.041 --> 00:40:38.590
and it was going from
side to side showing

00:40:38.591 --> 00:40:40.250
broad uncertainty estimates and

00:40:40.251 --> 00:40:41.520
so people actually turned it off.

00:40:41.521 --> 00:40:43.230
And the most recent,

00:40:43.231 --> 00:40:44.475
I think it was the midterms,

00:40:44.476 --> 00:40:47.160
not the midterms, but the
filling the vacancies.

00:40:47.161 --> 00:40:49.755
The New York Times didn't
have it anymore I think.

00:40:49.756 --> 00:40:51.830
So, people just has to be.

00:40:51.831 --> 00:40:53.950
&gt;&gt; We shouldn't discuss politics,

00:40:53.951 --> 00:40:56.565
and we do that in our peril
but see, there we go.

00:40:56.566 --> 00:40:57.690
&gt;&gt; You see.

00:40:57.691 --> 00:41:01.750
&gt;&gt; But I'm reminded of
the last presidential election,

00:41:01.751 --> 00:41:05.740
where at the last minute
Clinton was forecast to win

00:41:05.741 --> 00:41:07.240
by something like 70-30 and

00:41:07.241 --> 00:41:09.810
then when the unexpected
thing happened,

00:41:09.811 --> 00:41:11.650
everyone said, how were
the polls so wrong?

00:41:11.651 --> 00:41:12.780
It's like well, sometimes

00:41:12.781 --> 00:41:16.390
30 percent probability things
happen. It seems like.

00:41:16.391 --> 00:41:17.380
&gt;&gt; It happened exactly.

00:41:17.381 --> 00:41:19.550
&gt;&gt; Maybe if you have

00:41:19.551 --> 00:41:20.560
a well-calibrated model they

00:41:20.561 --> 00:41:21.760
should be happening
30 percent of the time.

00:41:21.761 --> 00:41:23.740
&gt;&gt; Exactly, I feel
really like that.

00:41:23.741 --> 00:41:25.450
&gt;&gt; Somehow people
don't even seem to

00:41:25.451 --> 00:41:27.520
think that way even
about binary choices.

00:41:27.521 --> 00:41:31.090
So, what hope do we have
about richer model spaces?

00:41:31.091 --> 00:41:34.970
&gt;&gt; I don't have an answer.
Does anybody else?

00:41:34.971 --> 00:41:38.780
&gt;&gt; Further, I think it's
going to be harder if

00:41:38.781 --> 00:41:43.500
you assign a human-like
nature to the system.

00:41:43.501 --> 00:41:45.345
So, especially robots or

00:41:45.346 --> 00:41:47.920
things that looked like
a human ride out car,

00:41:47.921 --> 00:41:50.830
that you think is being driven
by a human but it's not.

00:41:50.831 --> 00:41:52.310
So, you tend to assign

00:41:52.311 --> 00:41:53.770
the same qualities and

00:41:53.771 --> 00:41:56.915
the same behavioral
response from a human.

00:41:56.916 --> 00:41:59.360
So, there I think a lot of,

00:41:59.361 --> 00:42:01.865
maybe even if we give
uncertainty estimates,

00:42:01.866 --> 00:42:05.380
you kind of ignore all that
and you just go back to how

00:42:05.381 --> 00:42:07.190
you would expect a human to

00:42:07.191 --> 00:42:09.840
react and I think that's
even more dangerous.

00:42:09.841 --> 00:42:11.480
&gt;&gt; Well, I guess this comes

00:42:11.481 --> 00:42:13.020
back to your notion before of,

00:42:13.021 --> 00:42:14.970
objective functions and
also the point that Victor

00:42:14.971 --> 00:42:17.145
brought up before about
causal reasoning,

00:42:17.146 --> 00:42:18.795
about really being able to

00:42:18.796 --> 00:42:21.725
ascribe some causal motivation.

00:42:21.726 --> 00:42:23.780
It seems to me that
this is another place

00:42:23.781 --> 00:42:25.590
where Machine Learning
has been very fruitful,

00:42:25.591 --> 00:42:27.210
but also where there's an

00:42:27.211 --> 00:42:28.660
inherent tension
about reasoning from

00:42:28.661 --> 00:42:30.660
data because you want

00:42:30.661 --> 00:42:32.120
to understand something
counterfactual,

00:42:32.121 --> 00:42:33.440
you don't necessarily
want to understand

00:42:33.441 --> 00:42:35.220
only what the data
has to tell you.

00:42:35.221 --> 00:42:37.890
Is there an inherent limit there?

00:42:41.910 --> 00:42:45.980
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, it's
especially interesting how

00:42:45.981 --> 00:42:47.925
different fields think about

00:42:47.926 --> 00:42:49.790
the same questions in
a very different way.

00:42:49.791 --> 00:42:51.540
So, I spent two days
in Tubingen with

00:42:51.541 --> 00:42:54.450
Bernhard Scholkopf, and so,

00:42:54.451 --> 00:42:56.930
his lab is devoting

00:42:56.931 --> 00:42:59.260
all of its attention
out to questions of

00:42:59.261 --> 00:43:02.545
Causal Inference and then

00:43:02.546 --> 00:43:05.380
they want out to automate
the causal discoveries.

00:43:05.381 --> 00:43:07.210
But it seems like
it's really hard

00:43:07.211 --> 00:43:10.305
to do so without
contextual knowledge,

00:43:10.306 --> 00:43:13.940
without experts being
brought in and so on.

00:43:13.941 --> 00:43:16.470
Just this really simplistic

00:43:16.471 --> 00:43:17.770
approach of saying let's assume

00:43:17.771 --> 00:43:22.180
that we have a diag or
something like this,

00:43:22.181 --> 00:43:27.095
a diagram without cycles and
then they just discover it,

00:43:27.096 --> 00:43:28.420
it often goes wrong,

00:43:28.421 --> 00:43:31.070
like it would go wrong
in supply-demand systems

00:43:31.071 --> 00:43:33.520
because it's not really
a diag and so on.

00:43:33.521 --> 00:43:35.100
And without knowing the context,

00:43:35.101 --> 00:43:37.475
I think it could
go terribly wrong.

00:43:37.476 --> 00:43:40.655
So, I don't know how to
automate this and so on.

00:43:40.656 --> 00:43:43.600
People mentioned here
quantification of uncertainty here,

00:43:43.601 --> 00:43:46.650
I don't think we all agree on
what the uncertainty means.

00:43:46.651 --> 00:43:48.205
I would be surprised, again,

00:43:48.206 --> 00:43:50.020
if somebody has
a good quantification

00:43:50.021 --> 00:43:52.515
of uncertainty for
Deep Neural Nets.

00:43:52.516 --> 00:43:55.500
If you have a prediction
for based on them,

00:43:55.501 --> 00:43:57.180
I would be interested in seeing

00:43:57.181 --> 00:44:00.000
a confidence interval,
the classical one.

00:44:00.001 --> 00:44:03.210
The frequentist one
just to be clear.

00:44:05.280 --> 00:44:07.420
It's even for prediction,

00:44:07.421 --> 00:44:10.375
the counterfactual prediction
is much much harder

00:44:10.376 --> 00:44:13.870
because we have to
create unbiased signals

00:44:13.871 --> 00:44:17.310
about the treatment effects
or causal effects

00:44:17.311 --> 00:44:21.070
and the constructions of

00:44:21.071 --> 00:44:23.435
session by signals often
relies on experiments.

00:44:23.436 --> 00:44:25.340
Experiments are not available for

00:44:25.341 --> 00:44:28.395
the most interesting research
questions that we have.

00:44:28.396 --> 00:44:30.180
The experience can be used

00:44:30.181 --> 00:44:32.980
to measure short-run
effects that give you

00:44:32.981 --> 00:44:37.560
an idea about the true causes,

00:44:37.561 --> 00:44:38.970
but we're so far away

00:44:38.971 --> 00:44:45.320
from learning long-run effects

00:44:45.321 --> 00:44:48.290
and from automating the learning.

00:44:51.390 --> 00:44:54.160
&gt;&gt; All right. This is
the first thing that spurred

00:44:54.161 --> 00:44:57.220
our audience,
spontaneous activities.

00:44:58.070 --> 00:45:00.620
&gt;&gt; I think going back to
your question about how will we

00:45:00.621 --> 00:45:02.830
adapt to this
basically new world,

00:45:02.831 --> 00:45:04.970
I think we have to.

00:45:04.971 --> 00:45:07.755
But when I say we, I really mean

00:45:07.756 --> 00:45:10.040
the younger generation
because we are old.

00:45:10.041 --> 00:45:11.340
It's a new world.

00:45:11.960 --> 00:45:15.105
&gt;&gt; Why do you so little faith
in the older generation.

00:45:15.106 --> 00:45:16.490
&gt;&gt; It's a new world, we're old,

00:45:16.491 --> 00:45:18.735
but I think there is
hope for education.

00:45:18.736 --> 00:45:19.970
I think a lot of universities are

00:45:19.971 --> 00:45:21.305
already starting to integrate

00:45:21.306 --> 00:45:22.860
statistical education into

00:45:22.861 --> 00:45:25.035
the standard curriculum
for all students.

00:45:25.036 --> 00:45:27.150
I've seen Data Science
101 courses popping

00:45:27.151 --> 00:45:29.435
up at places like
Stanford and Berkeley.

00:45:29.436 --> 00:45:30.790
I think we need to
go farther back

00:45:30.791 --> 00:45:32.035
than that because
now we already have,

00:45:32.036 --> 00:45:33.780
I mean, these systems
are being deployed.

00:45:33.781 --> 00:45:35.675
We already have Machine Learning
systems that are just

00:45:35.676 --> 00:45:38.520
helping to decide who's going
to get released on bail.

00:45:38.521 --> 00:45:40.125
It's affecting your life

00:45:40.126 --> 00:45:41.755
your life and what's
going to happen,

00:45:41.756 --> 00:45:45.000
will you end up in jail or
released with your family?

00:45:45.001 --> 00:45:47.550
And that's going to
happen more and more.

00:45:47.551 --> 00:45:48.840
So, it means
the new generations will

00:45:48.841 --> 00:45:49.990
have to be educated
on these things.

00:45:49.991 --> 00:45:52.480
So, I think it is
difficult to take someone

00:45:52.481 --> 00:45:53.970
like me and to

00:45:53.971 --> 00:45:55.440
be retrained into thinking
about uncertainty,

00:45:55.441 --> 00:45:58.135
but I think if we
start early we can.

00:45:58.136 --> 00:46:00.150
&gt;&gt; So, we shouldn't
even have faith in

00:46:00.151 --> 00:46:02.795
former stats professors
from Stanford?

00:46:02.796 --> 00:46:05.160
There's no hope for
the rest of us.

00:46:05.161 --> 00:46:07.225
&gt;&gt; Point estimates,
catch them early.

00:46:07.226 --> 00:46:09.840
That should be the public health.

00:46:09.841 --> 00:46:11.960
&gt;&gt; So, should calculus in

00:46:11.961 --> 00:46:14.130
high schools be
replaced by statistics?

00:46:14.131 --> 00:46:15.305
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:46:15.306 --> 00:46:16.280
&gt;&gt; Augmented.

00:46:16.281 --> 00:46:18.245
&gt;&gt; Augmented, I would say.

00:46:18.246 --> 00:46:20.160
&gt;&gt; Now, what are you
going to kick out?

00:46:20.161 --> 00:46:21.250
&gt;&gt; Calculus.

00:46:21.251 --> 00:46:23.510
&gt;&gt; You can't say augmented.

00:46:24.450 --> 00:46:28.280
&gt;&gt; Stochastic calculus. Yeah.

00:46:29.640 --> 00:46:31.300
&gt;&gt; It's the best kind.

00:46:31.301 --> 00:46:32.930
&gt;&gt; I like that.

00:46:33.120 --> 00:46:37.805
&gt;&gt; Okay. So, we've talked
a bunch about the limits of ML,

00:46:37.806 --> 00:46:39.860
both how ML's going to grow,

00:46:39.861 --> 00:46:43.080
and also what maybe ultimately
is going to constrain it.

00:46:43.081 --> 00:46:44.900
If we imagine that all of

00:46:44.901 --> 00:46:46.630
our machine learning
dreams are realized,

00:46:46.631 --> 00:46:48.850
machine learning takes
its appropriate place at

00:46:48.851 --> 00:46:51.725
the center of computer science,
ruling the world,

00:46:51.726 --> 00:46:54.355
what other areas of
computer science

00:46:54.356 --> 00:46:57.110
and maybe of the sciences

00:46:57.111 --> 00:47:00.155
more generally are going
to increase in importance,

00:47:00.156 --> 00:47:03.330
because of their
inherent complementarity

00:47:03.331 --> 00:47:06.680
to the place the machine
learning will ultimately reach.

00:47:06.681 --> 00:47:08.980
I think this is particularly
important given that,

00:47:08.981 --> 00:47:11.820
MSL New England is
such an interdisciplinary place.

00:47:11.821 --> 00:47:14.030
So, we're here really to
celebrate MSL New England,

00:47:14.031 --> 00:47:18.485
and I think in the interdisciplinary
lab of the future.

00:47:18.486 --> 00:47:20.475
If you're going to
be centered on ML,

00:47:20.476 --> 00:47:22.390
who else do you need to
make sure you have around?.

00:47:22.391 --> 00:47:26.375
&gt;&gt; I mean. So, at
Caltech as well,

00:47:26.376 --> 00:47:28.875
it's a very interdisciplinary
place, right?

00:47:28.876 --> 00:47:31.230
We recently launched
AI for science

00:47:31.231 --> 00:47:33.915
with the goal of
really having AI,

00:47:33.916 --> 00:47:36.695
make an impact in
all areas of sciences.

00:47:36.696 --> 00:47:38.800
So, to me yes,

00:47:38.801 --> 00:47:42.130
there should be a central
overarching machine learning

00:47:42.131 --> 00:47:43.750
affecting all the fields,

00:47:43.751 --> 00:47:46.930
but ultimately it
also has to bring in

00:47:46.931 --> 00:47:48.340
all the domain experts
and to transfer some

00:47:48.341 --> 00:47:51.445
of the machine learning
knowledge to them,

00:47:51.446 --> 00:47:54.305
and for them to also further
develop machine learning.

00:47:54.306 --> 00:47:57.105
So it's not going to be a few of

00:47:57.106 --> 00:47:58.920
machine learning researchers just

00:47:58.921 --> 00:48:00.595
solving all the world's problems.

00:48:00.596 --> 00:48:02.570
I think that would be
also too arrogant to

00:48:02.571 --> 00:48:05.105
think that a few of
us could do that,

00:48:05.106 --> 00:48:07.170
but instead, they would really

00:48:07.171 --> 00:48:10.060
be machine learning itself
getting democratize,

00:48:10.061 --> 00:48:12.140
spreading its wings and

00:48:12.141 --> 00:48:15.975
the basic grounding is present
for all sciences in fact,

00:48:15.976 --> 00:48:20.180
all areas of education
including the arts.

00:48:20.181 --> 00:48:24.670
Then it's up to every researcher
in every field to say,

00:48:24.671 --> 00:48:26.560
''I know how is this going to be

00:48:26.561 --> 00:48:28.900
relevant to me and how
can I further develop,

00:48:28.901 --> 00:48:30.840
what are the domain
constraints I have,

00:48:30.841 --> 00:48:32.580
is it the small data regime,

00:48:32.581 --> 00:48:33.795
is it the large data,

00:48:33.796 --> 00:48:35.905
is it the interactive learning,

00:48:35.906 --> 00:48:39.420
is it mostly online learning."

00:48:39.421 --> 00:48:41.750
So, all these, like it
what aspect is really

00:48:41.751 --> 00:48:44.495
relevant to my field
and how do I value it.

00:48:44.496 --> 00:48:46.810
So, we have to really broaden

00:48:46.811 --> 00:48:50.140
machine learning rather
than centralized it.

00:48:50.880 --> 00:48:53.530
&gt;&gt; When I was graduate
student at Berkeley,

00:48:53.531 --> 00:48:56.520
I was part of
this interdisciplinary lab

00:48:56.521 --> 00:48:58.080
which was called the Rad Lab.

00:48:58.081 --> 00:49:00.120
So, it was essentially
machine- it

00:49:00.121 --> 00:49:02.350
was a system's plus
machine learning lab,

00:49:02.351 --> 00:49:04.270
but the goal of

00:49:04.271 --> 00:49:05.810
the machine learning piece
was to develop

00:49:05.811 --> 00:49:07.975
machinery that are
useful for systems.

00:49:07.976 --> 00:49:09.820
By the end of my time
in graduate school,

00:49:09.821 --> 00:49:11.695
I had been replaced by
new lab called the AMPLab,

00:49:11.696 --> 00:49:12.380
and again it was

00:49:12.381 --> 00:49:13.660
the Systems Engineering Lab
and then it was

00:49:13.661 --> 00:49:15.110
about building systems that

00:49:15.111 --> 00:49:16.690
will support machine learning.

00:49:16.691 --> 00:49:18.530
I think that's been
the crucial part two

00:49:18.531 --> 00:49:20.230
of scaling up machine learning,

00:49:20.231 --> 00:49:22.870
dealing with all the big
data problems that we have,

00:49:22.871 --> 00:49:25.104
making use of
parallel processing,

00:49:25.105 --> 00:49:26.480
dealing with cloud computing.

00:49:26.481 --> 00:49:28.410
So, systems in particular,
I think is one of the good.

00:49:28.411 --> 00:49:29.735
I think there are a number
of answers, but that's

00:49:29.736 --> 00:49:31.325
one particular area that we need.

00:49:31.326 --> 00:49:33.555
&gt;&gt; I think that that would
have been one of my answers to

00:49:33.556 --> 00:49:34.865
the first question
about what's been

00:49:34.866 --> 00:49:36.890
surprising in the last 10 years,

00:49:36.891 --> 00:49:39.300
is the extent to which the
breakthroughs in ML had been,

00:49:39.301 --> 00:49:40.510
systems breakthroughs, rather

00:49:40.511 --> 00:49:43.015
than the theory breakthroughs.

00:49:43.016 --> 00:49:45.890
&gt;&gt; I should say GPU serving.

00:49:45.891 --> 00:49:48.170
&gt;&gt; That's a soft spot
for you, for sure.

00:49:48.171 --> 00:49:51.750
&gt;&gt; Well, probably it's

00:49:51.751 --> 00:49:53.420
easy to see because
it's such a strength

00:49:53.421 --> 00:49:54.460
of the New England lab,

00:49:54.461 --> 00:49:56.800
but the combination of

00:49:56.801 --> 00:49:58.520
quantitatively minded people and

00:49:58.521 --> 00:50:02.280
qualitatively minded people
is a powerful one.

00:50:02.281 --> 00:50:06.640
So, I think humanists around
and engaging with them,

00:50:06.641 --> 00:50:11.840
is a crazy powerful combination

00:50:11.841 --> 00:50:14.530
and because sometimes machinery

00:50:14.531 --> 00:50:17.040
it's easy to get
tangled up in just,

00:50:17.041 --> 00:50:20.390
I can answer this
so why not do it.

00:50:20.391 --> 00:50:22.910
Sometimes it's useful to
direct your attention to

00:50:22.911 --> 00:50:25.100
what should you really answer,

00:50:25.101 --> 00:50:26.205
not just because you can,

00:50:26.206 --> 00:50:27.775
but it is because you should.

00:50:27.776 --> 00:50:29.580
Those usually are
the harder questions

00:50:29.581 --> 00:50:30.965
that are more profound,

00:50:30.966 --> 00:50:33.015
and it's useful to have people

00:50:33.016 --> 00:50:37.650
who be view in those ideas.

00:50:37.980 --> 00:50:41.260
&gt;&gt; To comment too
along that line.

00:50:41.261 --> 00:50:43.830
So, I know these days I give

00:50:43.831 --> 00:50:47.705
talks to broad audiences
and also around the world,

00:50:47.706 --> 00:50:51.160
really reach out to people

00:50:51.161 --> 00:50:54.070
but maybe not having
the math background,

00:50:54.071 --> 00:50:55.770
and so many of them very like,

00:50:55.771 --> 00:50:58.830
how do I participate in this
machine learning revolution?

00:50:58.831 --> 00:51:03.100
How do I- I want to learn
but where do I start?

00:51:03.101 --> 00:51:05.370
So, that's been
another great thing that

00:51:05.371 --> 00:51:07.390
our efforts like fast RTI

00:51:07.391 --> 00:51:09.580
and other community efforts that

00:51:09.581 --> 00:51:11.975
are really making it
easy for people to,

00:51:11.976 --> 00:51:13.950
not only deploy machine learning,

00:51:13.951 --> 00:51:15.535
but to write simple code,

00:51:15.536 --> 00:51:18.219
to have very accessible examples

00:51:18.220 --> 00:51:20.450
and I think it's
so crucial that we

00:51:20.451 --> 00:51:22.350
make the education about

00:51:22.351 --> 00:51:25.925
machine learning and
through Kerala even math.

00:51:25.926 --> 00:51:29.540
Like much more accessible to

00:51:29.541 --> 00:51:33.540
broad population who maybe
thinking in different ways.

00:51:33.541 --> 00:51:35.950
That's the only way we're
going to make progress

00:51:35.951 --> 00:51:39.770
by involving humanity in

00:51:39.771 --> 00:51:42.590
creating better
machine learning and

00:51:42.591 --> 00:51:46.450
designing better even forms
of machine learning.

00:51:46.451 --> 00:51:48.900
&gt;&gt; So, yeah. I would
like to give comment,

00:51:48.901 --> 00:51:51.040
I think online education
is important.

00:51:51.041 --> 00:51:53.720
I could say, I've

00:51:53.721 --> 00:51:54.680
benefited a lot from

00:51:54.681 --> 00:51:56.460
Andrew Ing's course
in deep learning,

00:51:56.461 --> 00:51:59.705
I just love the
programming and so on.

00:51:59.706 --> 00:52:04.995
Very easy to for a busy person
it's really nice too.

00:52:04.996 --> 00:52:11.050
Second, I think, the community
is really important,

00:52:11.051 --> 00:52:13.165
I really enjoyed
my time with MSR,

00:52:13.166 --> 00:52:17.545
you have great community
really open-minded people,

00:52:17.546 --> 00:52:19.660
coming from different fields
and working together,

00:52:19.661 --> 00:52:22.760
discussing and breaking
language barriers

00:52:22.761 --> 00:52:24.775
and so on, that was fantastic.

00:52:24.776 --> 00:52:27.080
In some ways, what we have now at

00:52:27.081 --> 00:52:28.680
the Center for statistics

00:52:28.681 --> 00:52:30.840
and Data science is
very similar in nature,

00:52:30.841 --> 00:52:31.870
you have different people

00:52:31.871 --> 00:52:33.540
coming from
different departments,

00:52:33.541 --> 00:52:37.170
working in stats in machine
learning, broadly defined.

00:52:37.171 --> 00:52:41.580
We created educational programs
like minor in stats,

00:52:41.581 --> 00:52:46.105
dual PhD program in
exons statistics,

00:52:46.106 --> 00:52:47.750
mathematics and statistics or

00:52:47.751 --> 00:52:50.630
economics and
statistics and so on.

00:52:50.940 --> 00:52:55.270
I think we have the best seminar,

00:52:55.271 --> 00:52:57.470
I believe it's called stochastic
in statistics seminary,

00:52:57.471 --> 00:53:00.985
I really love the seminar.

00:53:00.986 --> 00:53:02.470
Lots of people come in,

00:53:02.471 --> 00:53:06.360
it's really nice
presentations with theorems

00:53:06.361 --> 00:53:13.030
improve and so on [inaudible].

00:53:13.031 --> 00:53:16.000
We, yeah, started this new
under graduate program which

00:53:16.001 --> 00:53:19.120
is Bachelor of Science

00:53:19.121 --> 00:53:23.370
in Computer Science Economics
and Data science.

00:53:23.371 --> 00:53:25.830
So, I think that's going to

00:53:25.831 --> 00:53:29.990
be game changer for us at least.

00:53:29.991 --> 00:53:31.420
&gt;&gt; So, to connect
something that came

00:53:31.421 --> 00:53:32.680
up in a lot of
your different answers,

00:53:32.681 --> 00:53:34.985
I particularly liked what

00:53:34.986 --> 00:53:37.665
Nicholas said about
humanists being

00:53:37.666 --> 00:53:44.275
useful to this broader
enterprise of machine learning.

00:53:44.276 --> 00:53:47.070
You maybe connecting
to what Lester was

00:53:47.071 --> 00:53:49.695
saying about the new generation,

00:53:49.696 --> 00:53:51.550
but imagine that you're

00:53:51.551 --> 00:53:55.220
an aspiring academic in

00:53:55.221 --> 00:53:56.360
some area that you're

00:53:56.361 --> 00:53:58.125
never going to be
a machine-learning researcher,

00:53:58.126 --> 00:54:00.310
but you believe that machine
learning is going to

00:54:00.311 --> 00:54:03.045
be a really important part
of the future,

00:54:03.046 --> 00:54:05.320
maybe you're a social scientist,

00:54:05.321 --> 00:54:07.245
maybe you're a theorist,

00:54:07.246 --> 00:54:10.510
maybe you're a scientist
in some other discipline.

00:54:10.511 --> 00:54:12.285
What do you need
to do differently

00:54:12.286 --> 00:54:14.680
compared to maybe
the generation of

00:54:14.681 --> 00:54:16.310
academics before you in

00:54:16.311 --> 00:54:19.160
your own discipline to
be better positioned,

00:54:19.161 --> 00:54:20.520
to be working as

00:54:20.521 --> 00:54:21.930
part of this machine
learning enterprise,

00:54:21.931 --> 00:54:23.670
to be working
alongside ML people?

00:54:23.671 --> 00:54:26.080
&gt;&gt; The very first is the data.

00:54:26.081 --> 00:54:31.030
So, I would say, are

00:54:31.031 --> 00:54:34.720
you able put together the data
that's there in the field,

00:54:34.721 --> 00:54:36.745
maybe it's in disparate forms

00:54:36.746 --> 00:54:38.150
and maybe there's even

00:54:38.151 --> 00:54:39.490
missing ones, but
at the very least,

00:54:39.491 --> 00:54:42.960
can you try to bring that
all together and ask

00:54:42.961 --> 00:54:47.750
by combining the data sets
and even collecting new data,

00:54:47.751 --> 00:54:51.770
can I really now answer
questions I couldn't before.

00:54:51.771 --> 00:54:55.360
I'm working with
the machine learning scientists,

00:54:55.361 --> 00:54:57.824
I think it has to be
a close collaboration,

00:54:57.825 --> 00:55:00.060
because that's the only way

00:55:00.061 --> 00:55:03.675
both sides can learn
from each other.

00:55:03.676 --> 00:55:06.875
So, those are the two
things I would say.

00:55:06.876 --> 00:55:08.550
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I agree with that

00:55:08.551 --> 00:55:09.615
and I think the only
thing I would add

00:55:09.616 --> 00:55:12.520
was probably trying to
get a job from Jennifer.

00:55:17.070 --> 00:55:19.885
&gt;&gt; Working together on problems,

00:55:19.886 --> 00:55:22.260
you have a scientist that has

00:55:22.261 --> 00:55:25.300
a domain knowledge
and has a problem.

00:55:25.930 --> 00:55:30.600
He or she should be
able to approach and

00:55:30.601 --> 00:55:32.580
talk a machine-learning scientist

00:55:32.581 --> 00:55:34.595
to help solve the problem.

00:55:34.596 --> 00:55:37.430
There should be
a common educational base

00:55:37.431 --> 00:55:40.200
to be able to speak to get
on the same page quickly.

00:55:40.201 --> 00:55:41.390
&gt;&gt; Then, I guess I want to open

00:55:41.391 --> 00:55:42.610
that box a little bit though.

00:55:42.611 --> 00:55:44.490
I don't just want to talk
about the value of talking

00:55:44.491 --> 00:55:46.430
together or the value of
having to drop from Jennifer,

00:55:46.431 --> 00:55:49.770
I want the recipe for having
to drop from Jennifer.

00:55:49.771 --> 00:55:52.270
What is the skill set that
people are going to need?

00:55:52.271 --> 00:55:55.370
What are they going to have
to actually develop as

00:55:55.371 --> 00:55:57.260
a toolkit to be able

00:55:57.261 --> 00:55:59.910
to engage in those
collaborations usefully?

00:55:59.911 --> 00:56:01.780
&gt;&gt; So, at Caltech,

00:56:01.781 --> 00:56:04.110
we have now a new
Data Science major and

00:56:04.111 --> 00:56:07.490
minor that's across
campus anybody can take.

00:56:07.491 --> 00:56:09.675
So the minor, especially.

00:56:09.676 --> 00:56:10.960
So, I think augmenting like

00:56:10.961 --> 00:56:13.760
the core area are
the core major with

00:56:13.761 --> 00:56:15.220
a minor in Data Science would be

00:56:15.221 --> 00:56:18.040
a great start because you
have the fundamentals.

00:56:18.041 --> 00:56:19.960
If you are out of school too,

00:56:19.961 --> 00:56:22.360
do the online courses too,

00:56:22.361 --> 00:56:23.935
I tell a lot of

00:56:23.936 --> 00:56:25.550
candidates that I get who

00:56:25.551 --> 00:56:27.110
want to get into
machine learning,

00:56:27.111 --> 00:56:28.680
I point them to the resources.

00:56:28.681 --> 00:56:30.265
These Jupyter
Notebooks are great,

00:56:30.266 --> 00:56:32.390
they have explanations
but they also

00:56:32.391 --> 00:56:34.690
have working code
to really hands-on,

00:56:34.691 --> 00:56:36.575
tried on different datasets,

00:56:36.576 --> 00:56:38.625
explore the failure modes.

00:56:38.626 --> 00:56:41.710
I think that's what really
distinguishes somebody with

00:56:41.711 --> 00:56:43.430
a surface knowledge
of machine learning

00:56:43.431 --> 00:56:45.160
and somebody who is deeper is,

00:56:45.161 --> 00:56:47.220
okay, why are things
failing, how do I debug,

00:56:47.221 --> 00:56:48.580
how do I resolve

00:56:48.581 --> 00:56:52.120
that and to participate
in online forums,

00:56:52.121 --> 00:56:54.265
I mean there's just
so much activity happening

00:56:54.266 --> 00:56:57.050
online and even in
meet-ups and workshops.

00:56:57.051 --> 00:56:58.230
So, there's a lot of

00:56:58.231 --> 00:57:00.460
community-driven efforts and I

00:57:00.461 --> 00:57:03.320
think being part of it
would be a great start.

00:57:03.321 --> 00:57:06.070
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Let me
give you an example

00:57:06.071 --> 00:57:08.605
that I've seen with my own eyes.

00:57:08.606 --> 00:57:10.210
During my PhD, I was in

00:57:10.211 --> 00:57:12.655
the Institute for
Transition Neuroscience.

00:57:12.656 --> 00:57:14.260
So, we were

00:57:14.261 --> 00:57:16.795
a machine-learning group
embedded with biologists.

00:57:16.796 --> 00:57:19.575
These biologists
received training,

00:57:19.576 --> 00:57:21.560
some of them like 10 years ago,

00:57:21.561 --> 00:57:23.770
12 years ago at the time.

00:57:23.771 --> 00:57:26.960
They went through
stats and math courses

00:57:26.961 --> 00:57:30.140
and basic computer science
courses but for a lot of them,

00:57:30.141 --> 00:57:34.880
it was not a core coursework.

00:57:34.881 --> 00:57:36.820
So, it was just pass or fail.

00:57:36.821 --> 00:57:40.320
Yeah, you passed stats, good job.

00:57:40.321 --> 00:57:41.870
So, then they realized and

00:57:41.871 --> 00:57:43.350
then there was this
machine-learning group and

00:57:43.351 --> 00:57:47.325
our goal was to work at
the intersection of fields.

00:57:47.326 --> 00:57:49.695
They would come with
their microarray data,

00:57:49.696 --> 00:57:51.310
at the time it was microarrays.

00:57:51.311 --> 00:57:52.800
They would say,
"Okay, I will need

00:57:52.801 --> 00:57:54.440
to understand
differential expression

00:57:54.441 --> 00:57:56.310
between a brain tissue that

00:57:56.311 --> 00:57:57.050
has the disease and

00:57:57.051 --> 00:57:58.940
brain tissue that doesn't
have the disease."

00:57:58.941 --> 00:58:01.795
Being machinery people,
we pop something,

00:58:01.796 --> 00:58:03.860
calculate the p-value
and whatnot.

00:58:03.861 --> 00:58:06.345
Then they realized that
the turnaround time was long,

00:58:06.346 --> 00:58:08.200
that we maybe didn't
super look at

00:58:08.201 --> 00:58:11.320
calibration of the p-values,

00:58:11.321 --> 00:58:13.970
that sometimes when
you fit things,

00:58:13.971 --> 00:58:15.744
you do multiple comparisons,

00:58:15.745 --> 00:58:18.125
we were controlling but you
could control in better ways.

00:58:18.126 --> 00:58:19.990
So then they'll say, "Okay,
so these people are busy,

00:58:19.991 --> 00:58:22.580
they're doing
their machinery papers.

00:58:22.581 --> 00:58:24.290
Why don't we pick
it up ourselves?"

00:58:24.291 --> 00:58:27.505
So, they started getting
the wrong training stats

00:58:27.506 --> 00:58:29.150
and we rapidly found

00:58:29.151 --> 00:58:31.130
that once they got
these training stats,

00:58:31.131 --> 00:58:32.620
they would be super

00:58:32.621 --> 00:58:34.430
competitive with what
we could come up with.

00:58:34.431 --> 00:58:36.070
We can basically get
95 percent all the

00:58:36.071 --> 00:58:38.010
way there but because
they had the training,

00:58:38.011 --> 00:58:40.455
then they could communicate
more effectively with us,

00:58:40.456 --> 00:58:43.745
what if with their wishes
were from the system.

00:58:43.746 --> 00:58:45.930
At that point, there was

00:58:45.931 --> 00:58:47.540
this feedback loop where we can

00:58:47.541 --> 00:58:49.430
understand better what
they were looking for,

00:58:49.431 --> 00:58:51.350
they understood better
what our models were

00:58:51.351 --> 00:58:53.430
doing and so they learn
more machine learning,

00:58:53.431 --> 00:58:55.530
we learn more biology.

00:58:55.531 --> 00:58:58.890
That was like a virtual cycle.

00:58:58.891 --> 00:59:00.550
&gt;&gt; I'm a bit

00:59:00.551 --> 00:59:02.810
surprised that so far the
answers you guys are giving are

00:59:02.811 --> 00:59:04.310
about how people in
other disciplines

00:59:04.311 --> 00:59:05.680
should learn some machine

00:59:05.681 --> 00:59:06.950
learning themselves,
instead of you-

00:59:06.951 --> 00:59:08.470
&gt;&gt; No, I said stats,
not machine learning.

00:59:08.471 --> 00:59:11.400
&gt;&gt; Oh, stats but you should
learn the toolkit that

00:59:11.401 --> 00:59:14.775
I know people have to
meet in the middle.

00:59:14.776 --> 00:59:17.550
I guess as you're wearing
an economics hat,

00:59:17.551 --> 00:59:19.760
I'm particularly excited by
the idea of compliments.

00:59:19.761 --> 00:59:22.730
What are entirely
different things

00:59:22.731 --> 00:59:23.880
where people might lean in

00:59:23.881 --> 00:59:25.490
an opposite direction to really

00:59:25.491 --> 00:59:28.500
develop a skill set that I
know people don't have but

00:59:28.501 --> 00:59:30.005
that's going to be
really relevant

00:59:30.006 --> 00:59:31.675
in a world with
a lot of ML people?

00:59:31.676 --> 00:59:33.850
&gt;&gt; Human interaction.

00:59:35.610 --> 00:59:38.930
So, I think that I mean
to be more serious,

00:59:38.931 --> 00:59:41.325
it'll be really
important in the future.

00:59:41.326 --> 00:59:43.840
So, it's not just
a mathematical ability

00:59:43.841 --> 00:59:45.600
but to communicate

00:59:45.601 --> 00:59:50.320
to different areas to empathize
with the difficulties,

00:59:50.321 --> 00:59:52.250
the different sections
of population have

00:59:52.251 --> 00:59:55.065
or different fields
or phasing so how

00:59:55.066 --> 01:00:01.305
we can learn from others to
help solve better problems.

01:00:01.306 --> 01:00:03.390
&gt;&gt; This was a tough
question because it

01:00:03.391 --> 01:00:05.025
was a many-to-one relation.

01:00:05.026 --> 01:00:06.510
If you define the set of all

01:00:06.511 --> 01:00:08.180
possible other fields
and then you say,

01:00:08.181 --> 01:00:11.950
"What's the compliment that
machinery doesn't have?"

01:00:11.951 --> 01:00:15.865
It's a really tough
set to define.

01:00:15.866 --> 01:00:18.195
So, I don't have an answer.

01:00:18.196 --> 01:00:20.620
&gt;&gt; Well, you're almost out
of time, so fair enough.

01:00:20.621 --> 01:00:22.470
&gt;&gt; Well, I say I feel
like machine learning is

01:00:22.471 --> 01:00:23.940
largely like a field
of immigrants.

01:00:23.941 --> 01:00:26.560
I mean, being
a pretty young field.

01:00:26.561 --> 01:00:27.675
A lot of these people came in

01:00:27.676 --> 01:00:31.220
from other disciplines
in computer science,

01:00:31.221 --> 01:00:34.630
or mathematics, from
physics quite often.

01:00:36.020 --> 01:00:38.480
I guess say I can disagree with

01:00:38.481 --> 01:00:40.325
the rest of the question
and then I do think

01:00:40.326 --> 01:00:41.450
the best thing for them is to

01:00:41.451 --> 01:00:42.660
go and learn machine learning

01:00:42.661 --> 01:00:46.040
too and then augment
their own skill set with that.

01:00:46.041 --> 01:00:49.115
Yeah, that's how I'd approach it.

01:00:49.116 --> 01:00:51.370
&gt;&gt; Well, if I can't get you
to disagree with each other,

01:00:51.371 --> 01:00:53.750
at least I can get you
to disagree with me.

01:00:53.751 --> 01:00:57.745
So, we're just in
the last seconds left.

01:00:57.746 --> 01:00:59.490
Let me just invite
you each to give

01:00:59.491 --> 01:01:02.000
some closing thoughts
if you have any.

01:01:02.001 --> 01:01:03.640
How do we get there from here?

01:01:03.641 --> 01:01:04.800
What are the things we should all

01:01:04.801 --> 01:01:06.130
be focusing on as a group

01:01:06.131 --> 01:01:07.910
to realize the vision that we

01:01:07.911 --> 01:01:10.830
spent the last hour
talking about?

01:01:10.831 --> 01:01:13.690
&gt;&gt; I mean for me, it
would be bridging

01:01:13.691 --> 01:01:16.600
theory and practice to ask,

01:01:16.601 --> 01:01:19.800
what are the current
blind spots in practice?

01:01:19.801 --> 01:01:22.010
Can we design better algorithms?

01:01:22.011 --> 01:01:25.400
Also, really explore
the failure modes

01:01:25.401 --> 01:01:27.580
of those algorithms
and keep iterating.

01:01:27.581 --> 01:01:32.910
So, it's always go on

01:01:32.911 --> 01:01:35.520
but to make it much
more of a process

01:01:35.521 --> 01:01:39.410
that's easy to do and
to focus more on data.

01:01:40.440 --> 01:01:43.725
&gt;&gt; At least for me,
the most important things

01:01:43.726 --> 01:01:47.160
is breaking gaps between
theory and practice,

01:01:47.161 --> 01:01:49.120
there's just a gigantic
gap out there.

01:01:49.121 --> 01:01:52.950
The second is breaking
communication barriers.

01:01:52.951 --> 01:01:55.910
There's a lot of
Data Science activities,

01:01:55.911 --> 01:01:59.280
we need to understand
each other better.

01:01:59.281 --> 01:02:01.830
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'd like to see
a broader dissemination

01:02:01.831 --> 01:02:03.500
of problems.

01:02:03.501 --> 01:02:06.200
I think research seems
to be a bit myopic.

01:02:06.201 --> 01:02:07.520
We come up with a problem,

01:02:07.521 --> 01:02:08.930
we try to solve it ourselves,
we can't solve it,

01:02:08.931 --> 01:02:11.770
we shelve it and
the problem dies,

01:02:11.771 --> 01:02:14.025
or maybe it's rediscovered
a decade later.

01:02:14.026 --> 01:02:16.990
Quite often, we might

01:02:16.991 --> 01:02:20.120
know what the problems are
but not the right solutions.

01:02:22.830 --> 01:02:25.240
In our current environment,
it's very easy

01:02:25.241 --> 01:02:27.390
to reach out to
the broad community.

01:02:27.391 --> 01:02:28.610
We already start to
see this with machine

01:02:28.611 --> 01:02:30.065
learning competitions,
for instance,

01:02:30.066 --> 01:02:31.860
where someone knows there's

01:02:31.861 --> 01:02:33.240
a particular problem
that needs to be solved.

01:02:33.241 --> 01:02:34.500
They're willing to
put out lots of

01:02:34.501 --> 01:02:35.960
money so somebody
else can solve it,

01:02:35.961 --> 01:02:37.270
and you end up finding solutions

01:02:37.271 --> 01:02:39.830
from places where you
wouldn't normally look,

01:02:39.831 --> 01:02:41.710
often from different disciplines.

01:02:41.711 --> 01:02:43.995
I think that's probably
the way forward to making

01:02:43.996 --> 01:02:47.960
the biggest contributions
to humanity, broadly.

01:02:48.200 --> 01:02:51.340
&gt;&gt; My main point is we should be

01:02:51.341 --> 01:02:52.535
worrying more about the

01:02:52.536 --> 01:02:54.140
good scholarship in
machine learning.

01:02:54.141 --> 01:02:57.050
So, there is this great paper
that is on archive,

01:02:57.051 --> 01:02:58.570
which is troubling trends

01:02:58.571 --> 01:03:00.310
in machine-learning scholarship.

01:03:00.311 --> 01:03:04.110
We are not always doing
a great job disentangling,

01:03:04.111 --> 01:03:05.610
for instance, the sources of

01:03:05.611 --> 01:03:08.135
an empirical game in performance.

01:03:08.136 --> 01:03:09.800
We should be thinking
more about that.

01:03:09.801 --> 01:03:12.370
So, people should read
that paper and think about it;

01:03:12.371 --> 01:03:14.770
it's a very interesting concept.

01:03:14.771 --> 01:03:18.550
&gt;&gt; Great, let's thank
all of our panelists

01:03:18.551 --> 01:03:24.360
for the enlightening facts.

